WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Hidden away in the vastness of the Pacific,

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.000
and undiscovered by men until only a few centuries ago,

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.000
lies a group of strange volcanic islands.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:25.000
Each is a crucible where evolution proceeds at extraordinary speed.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Galapagos

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Galapagos

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.000
Each island contains its own unique community.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.000
The discovery of these creatures

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.000
inspired an idea that changed our understanding of life on Earth.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Evolution.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:18.000
And today, scientists from Galapagos are continuing to make discoveries

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.000
that shed light on that crucial process

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.000
and have revealed that human beings can be just as powerful

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.000
a driving force for change as any other factor.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.000
Galapagos

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Something here was the catalyst

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:55.000
that produced the most spectacular explosion of biological diversity in the world.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.000
It's not just the number of species that appeared,

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.000
but the rate at which they did so.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.000
And the result is so extraordinary,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.000
it inspired the most important of all biological theories,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Charles Darwin's evolution by natural selection.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.000
And now, 200 years later,

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.000
we are beginning to understand the deep-lying forces

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:25.000
that produced this biological wonderland.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Isolation

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.000
One of those factors is isolation.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:46.000
Isolation

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.000
Isolation

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.000
A part of Isabella Island, the largest of the 16 in the archipelago,

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.000
is so difficult to get to, it's hard to ever visit.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.000
The Wolf Volcano

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:20.760
Its giant crater lies exactly on the equator and is encircled internally by steep cliffs.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:29.200
And on its flanks, evidence was discovered of a catastrophe that might, paradoxically,

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:35.720
eventually lead to the creation of a new species.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:42.000
This flow of recently solidified lava has created an impassable barrier a hundred metres

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.080
wide right through the vegetation.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:55.080
The fresh lava is razor sharp and almost impossible to cross.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:04:02.080
At the bottom of the crater are the bones.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:10.280
Bleached white by the baking sun, they are those of giant tortoises.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:17.280
Some animals that got stuck here are still clinging to life.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:31.360
Tortoises are tough. They can survive for months without any food or water. And that

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:38.360
worsened their fate. It will take them months to die.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:53.120
Why so many tortoises tried to cross the lava barrier, nobody knows. What drove them

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:57.480
to keep attempting this impossible journey?

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:04.480
Perhaps they were desperately trying to rejoin other members of their group.

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:09.480
Their deaths, however, are significant.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:15.480
They illustrate a principle that applies to the whole of nature. It's not unique to the

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:22.480
Galapagos, but it's because of the Galapagos that we first came to understand it.

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:29.480
This tragic scene filmed for the first time may provide a clue as to how a new species

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:35.120
may start to evolve.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:42.120
The lava flow created an impossible physical barrier across the tortoises' territory,

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:49.160
dividing it into two.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:56.160
So two tortoise populations that were once one must now live apart.

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:02.040
If there is any significant difference now or in the future between their two territories,

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:09.040
the tortoises may eventually become two different species.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:16.040
Animals and plants are evolving throughout the natural world.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:28.840
But it was the strange creatures of Galapagos that first revealed how that happens.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:36.200
Nearly 200 years ago, the islands were visited by a young naturalist.

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:43.200
On September 16, 1835, HMS Beagle arrived in the Galapagos Islands, and the 26-year-old

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:49.440
Charles Darwin stepped ashore to explore.

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:56.440
At the time, very little was known about the natural history of the islands. Darwin, initially,

00:06:56.440 --> 00:07:03.440
was fascinated by its geology. But it was the animals that gave him his historic insight.

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:19.200
Darwin was only on these islands for five weeks, but in that short time, there were

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:26.200
things he saw and conversations he had which stuck in his mind. For example, the British

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:32.200
vice-governor of Floriana Island said that he could tell which island a giant tortoise

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:39.200
came from simply by the shape of its shell.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:46.200
He pondered on the vice-governor's casual remark. Why were populations of tortoises

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:57.280
on separate islands all slightly different from one another?

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:04.280
He set about making a collection of animals and plants from all the islands he visited.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:21.480
Although it was the tortoises that first alerted Darwin to the differences between

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:28.480
animals on different islands, it was his collection of these undramatic little birds, the finches,

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:36.280
which provided him with the most substantial evidence for his great theory.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:42.600
We now know that the ancestral Galapagos finches arrived in these islands about two million

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:49.600
years ago. Since then, they have diversified into a number of different species. Today,

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:56.600
there are 13 of them distributed throughout the archipelago. Each has its own special

00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:04.160
talents. The woodpecker finch has discovered how to use a tool to wrinkle grubs out of

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:11.840
their burrows in the branches of trees. The vampire finch has learned how to extract blood

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:18.840
from sitting birds. Darwin, when he returned to England, brought back with him a wide variety

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:27.880
of specimens of all kinds. And he spent years studying his collections. He had a range of

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:36.320
finches from several of the islands. The finches were very popular in the early 19th century.

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:43.320
He had a range of finches from several of the islands, and he noticed one particular

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:51.400
way in which they differed. They had beaks of different sizes. Why? An idea grew in his

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:58.400
mind. It would also apply to tortoises, maybe to all animals and plants wherever they occurred.

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:10.800
Painstakingly, meticulously, he started to accumulate evidence from all over the world

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:17.800
to support his idea. He was very interested in the idea of a finch. He was very interested

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:24.800
in usingkuushas which, led to variation in thickness of the dizeries by spoon viewers.

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:27.800
evolution by natural selection.

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:36.800
And nowhere is its workings more vividly evident

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:40.800
than here, where it first occurred to him, in the Galapagos.

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:46.800
He realised why it was that there were several species of giant tortoises.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:51.800
That original species probably had a high domed shell like this one.

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:55.800
And that's very useful on well-watered islands like this,

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:58.800
where you have to barge your way through the vegetation.

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:02.800
But on other islands, there are other problems.

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:13.800
In the South East Corridor,

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:18.800
in the South East corner of the archipelago lies Espanola Island.

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:29.800
Here, there is virtually no edible vegetation at all.

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:36.800
Except, that is, for this prickly pear cactus, Opuntia.

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:41.800
But this species of Opuntia is very tall.

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:44.800
And it has a tough, woody trunk.

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:49.800
The only parts worth eating are the fleshy leaves and flowers at the top.

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:54.800
Any giant tortoise that could reach them could get a meal.

00:11:56.800 --> 00:12:01.800
Tortoises with low round fronts to their shells couldn't do that.

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:08.800
But those with a peak at the front of the shell and long necks

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:11.800
could just manage it.

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:15.800
So they were the ones that survived and produced young.

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:23.800
Over many thousands of generations and millions of years,

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:28.800
the shell shape of the Espanola tortoise became more and more exaggerated.

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:36.800
Now, the peak at the front of the shell

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:38.800
is shaped like a saddle.

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:48.800
Such a change didn't happen just on Espanola.

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:51.800
Different islands had their own versions.

00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:58.800
Eventually, there were 15 different species in the islands,

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:01.800
all descended from a single founder.

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:05.800
But why should the environments of the islands be so different?

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:11.800
Well, a hint of that reason may come from looking at films

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:14.800
I shot right here back in 1978.

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:20.800
In these programmes, we're going to survey the unmeasurable variety

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:23.800
of animals that have been produced by natural selection

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:27.800
and look at them not as isolated as they were in the past.

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:33.800
That image of me shot 30 years ago indicates something extraordinary.

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:39.800
In that time, the rock on which I was sitting has moved its position

00:13:39.800 --> 00:13:45.800
by about a metre from where I was then to where I am now.

00:13:45.800 --> 00:13:50.800
In fact, the whole of this island is drifting across the surface of the globe

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:54.800
at a rate of about 1.5 metres per second.

00:13:54.800 --> 00:14:00.800
I'm drifting across the surface of the globe at a rate of about 3 centimetres a year,

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:03.800
roughly the rate at which my fingernails grow.

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:08.800
That may not sound much, but in the three million-odd years

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:12.800
since this island emerged above the surface of the ocean,

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:18.800
it has drifted in a southeasterly direction by about 60 miles.

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:23.800
The movement in the Earth's crust are the key to understanding

00:14:23.800 --> 00:14:27.800
the archipelago's extraordinary evolutionary history.

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:32.800
A giant hotspot rising from the Earth's molten core

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:49.800
began to build the Galapagos four million years ago.

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:54.800
But as the island drifted away from it, other volcanoes replaced it,

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:58.800
one after the other.

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:05.800
Each was built from an accumulation of ash and lava.

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:11.800
But then, as each moved away, eruptions ceased.

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:24.800
So a group of islands appeared, one after the other.

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:27.800
The islands are separated from one another by water,

00:15:27.800 --> 00:15:31.800
so their populations can't, for the most part, mix.

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:34.800
But they're just close enough to each other

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:38.800
that they can be seen in the distance.

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:42.800
They can't mix, but they're just close enough

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:45.800
for an occasional animal to float across

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:50.800
and so seed a newly emerging island.

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:53.800
Because the islands are of different ages,

00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:59.800
they contain between them a great variety of environments.

00:15:59.800 --> 00:16:07.800
And each has molded its inhabitants in its own way.

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:18.800
That is why their animals are so diverse.

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:36.800
Each is a separate evolutionary community.

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:44.800
Darwin had noticed some of the clearest differences.

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:49.800
But there are many others that are less obvious.

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:52.800
An isolated population of animals can change

00:16:52.800 --> 00:17:05.800
not only in their anatomy, but in their behavior.

00:17:05.800 --> 00:17:13.800
Little lizards like this are found throughout the archipelago.

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:20.800
Each island has its own distinct species.

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:23.800
And they differ not so much in the way they look

00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:33.800
as the way they behave.

00:17:33.800 --> 00:17:37.800
This is a lava lizard.

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:41.800
There are lots of them on the rocks around here.

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:44.800
And in the breeding season, which is now,

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:48.800
the males are competing with one another,

00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:53.800
both for territory and for females.

00:17:53.800 --> 00:17:58.800
And the way they do so is with press-ups.

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:07.800
Watch.

00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:12.800
Actually, this is a model that is used by scientists

00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:16.800
to investigate the way in which these lizards

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:19.800
communicate with one another.

00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:33.800
Let's see how he gets on.

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:36.800
And there's a response.

00:18:36.800 --> 00:18:41.800
These press-ups vary, both in the number and the intensity,

00:18:41.800 --> 00:18:50.800
the speed at which they do it, and how high they bob their heads.

00:18:50.800 --> 00:18:55.800
The interesting thing is that the responses vary

00:18:55.800 --> 00:18:58.800
from species to species.

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:08.800
In other words, each species has its own language of justice.

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:11.800
There are slight physical differences

00:19:11.800 --> 00:19:17.800
between species of lava lizard on different islands.

00:19:17.800 --> 00:19:21.800
But now, because they have developed different gestures,

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:25.800
they can't interbreed even if they meet.

00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:37.800
They're separated by a language barrier.

00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:41.800
New technology now enables scientists

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:44.800
to investigate the workings of evolution

00:19:44.800 --> 00:19:53.800
in ways that Darwin could hardly have ever imagined.

00:19:53.800 --> 00:19:57.800
X-rays are being used to work out what's happening

00:19:57.800 --> 00:20:00.800
to one of the least impressive animals on the island,

00:20:00.800 --> 00:20:06.800
a tiny land snail.

00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:08.800
It's been known for a long time

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:11.800
that there are many different snail species,

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:17.800
all closely related.

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:21.800
But a recent study of their shells using X-rays

00:20:21.800 --> 00:20:28.800
has demonstrated just how diverse they have become.

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:32.800
The length of the shell compared to the diameter of the tube inside

00:20:32.800 --> 00:20:38.800
is marginally different in different populations.

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:44.800
It's certainly different enough to define them as different species.

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:47.800
And there is an extraordinary large number of them

00:20:47.800 --> 00:20:49.800
throughout the islands,

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:54.800
with what seem to be very complex interrelationships.

00:20:54.800 --> 00:20:57.800
In fact, there are more species of them

00:20:57.800 --> 00:21:01.800
than any other kind of animal on the islands.

00:21:01.800 --> 00:21:06.800
Even one small island may contain several different species.

00:21:06.800 --> 00:21:10.800
But why?

00:21:10.800 --> 00:21:13.800
It's all to do with the different microclimates

00:21:13.800 --> 00:21:20.800
that occur even within a single small island.

00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:24.800
Black lava rock has one species,

00:21:24.800 --> 00:21:31.800
and sandy beaches close by a very different one.

00:21:31.800 --> 00:21:34.800
A dark cave has one sort,

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:39.800
and a leafy forest growing around its mouth yet another kind.

00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:42.800
And it seems to be connected with humidity.

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:48.800
At the one extreme, there are these with a wide mouth.

00:21:48.800 --> 00:21:52.800
They come from relatively well-watered islands,

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:59.800
and a wide mouth like that has a body which produces a roughly globular shell.

00:21:59.800 --> 00:22:05.800
But on dry islands, the mouth has to be very small to prevent evaporation,

00:22:05.800 --> 00:22:10.800
and that produces a long conical shape.

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:16.800
So Galapagos's new species range from the subtly different, like snails,

00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:21.800
to the obvious, like the marine iguanas.

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:27.800
In other parts of the world, evolution usually proceeds in a slow, gradual way.

00:22:27.800 --> 00:22:33.800
It can take millions of years for a new species to appear.

00:22:33.800 --> 00:22:44.800
But in Galapagos, it's been happening in an evolutionally blink of an eye.

00:22:44.800 --> 00:22:52.800
Galapagos, for its size, has more unique species than anywhere else on Earth,

00:22:52.800 --> 00:23:03.800
and all have appeared in the island's comparatively short history.

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:08.800
And that raises another intriguing question.

00:23:08.800 --> 00:23:14.800
Why did such a great number appear so quickly?

00:23:14.800 --> 00:23:24.800
The answer is to do with the absence of some animals.

00:23:24.800 --> 00:23:27.800
Predators.

00:23:27.800 --> 00:23:33.800
The few that are here are all very small.

00:23:33.800 --> 00:23:40.800
This is one of the most lethal, Scolopendra, a centipede.

00:23:40.800 --> 00:23:45.800
It roams the undergrowth searching for prey, which it kills by injecting venom

00:23:45.800 --> 00:23:49.800
from the sharp claws on its front legs.

00:23:49.800 --> 00:23:57.800
But it's only 30 centimeters long, so its victims are mostly tiny.

00:23:57.800 --> 00:24:05.800
Bigger animals like mockingbirds can easily subdue one.

00:24:05.800 --> 00:24:16.800
They even feed centipedes to their chicks.

00:24:16.800 --> 00:24:20.800
The greatest concentration of predators in the whole archipelago

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:25.800
is found on the rocky island of Fernandina.

00:24:25.800 --> 00:24:32.800
But they, too, of their kind, are rather small and comparatively ineffectual.

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:36.800
Snakes.

00:24:36.800 --> 00:24:41.800
The Galapagos racer.

00:24:41.800 --> 00:24:49.800
They search for prey in the colonies of marine iguanas.

00:24:49.800 --> 00:24:54.800
But all they can manage to do is to pick off the young, the weak, and the dying.

00:24:54.800 --> 00:24:59.800
And even that they find quite difficult.

00:24:59.800 --> 00:25:06.800
They hunt in the rocks around the fringes of the colony.

00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:17.800
They have to use a combination of both venom and constriction to make a kill.

00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:32.800
And even then, because they're small snakes, it may take more than one to overcome a victim.

00:25:32.800 --> 00:25:48.800
And then there's a tug of war between the winners to settle who has the prize.

00:25:48.800 --> 00:25:57.800
There are other predators that prey on the iguanas from the air.

00:25:57.800 --> 00:26:08.800
The Galapagos hawk.

00:26:08.800 --> 00:26:15.800
But even this hunter is seldom powerful enough to subdue a big male.

00:26:15.800 --> 00:26:35.800
It usually waits until it finds one that is weak or encumbered.

00:26:35.800 --> 00:26:54.800
A pregnant female trying to find a place to lay her eggs.

00:26:54.800 --> 00:26:57.800
And there are not many hawks here.

00:26:57.800 --> 00:27:06.800
Only 150 mated pairs, because there's only a limited number of nesting sites.

00:27:06.800 --> 00:27:14.800
There are so few birds that they make very little impact on the iguana population.

00:27:14.800 --> 00:27:20.800
So why are there no larger predators on Galapagos?

00:27:20.800 --> 00:27:28.800
Most of the Galapagos animals came from the rainforests of South America, 600 miles away.

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:40.800
There are plenty of big predators in these forests.

00:27:40.800 --> 00:27:50.800
Reptiles got to Galapagos by floating across the ocean on rafts of vegetation.

00:27:50.800 --> 00:28:02.800
Only hardy animals like the iguanas and tortoises could make that 600-mile voyage.

00:28:02.800 --> 00:28:27.800
The great predators of the jungles, however, are mammals. Big, fast and warm-blooded, like jaguars.

00:28:27.800 --> 00:28:36.800
If any of them had ever been carried away on a raft of vegetation, they would have perished out on the open ocean within a few days.

00:28:36.800 --> 00:28:47.800
So now, while there are many kinds of herbivorous reptiles in the Galapagos, there are no large predatory mammals of any kind.

00:28:47.800 --> 00:28:54.800
And this has had a profound effect on the animals that did get here.

00:28:54.800 --> 00:29:01.800
Something noticed by every visitor who comes to the islands.

00:29:01.800 --> 00:29:09.800
All the animals here are amazingly tame.

00:29:09.800 --> 00:29:17.800
Even the little finches are happy to bathe within inches of a stranger.

00:29:17.800 --> 00:29:22.800
The lack of predators may have a surprisingly widespread effect.

00:29:22.800 --> 00:29:28.800
Not just that animals are not frightened of strangers, the so-called island tameness,

00:29:28.800 --> 00:29:37.800
but that time that would be spent hiding from attackers can now be used to find food, find mates and raise young.

00:29:37.800 --> 00:29:49.800
And so produce more young, which hastens the progress of evolution.

00:29:49.800 --> 00:29:56.800
There is no more impressive example of that than Fernandina's iguana colony.

00:29:56.800 --> 00:30:04.800
With no significant predators around, these herbivores produce lots of young.

00:30:04.800 --> 00:30:12.800
So many that their problem is not how to defend themselves, but how to find enough food to support their great numbers.

00:30:12.800 --> 00:30:20.800
So they ventured into the sea itself to graze seaweed on the seafloor.

00:30:20.800 --> 00:30:25.800
And although swimming in the cold sea cools them uncomfortably,

00:30:25.800 --> 00:30:39.800
with no predators around they can soon put that right by stretching out in the sun.

00:30:39.800 --> 00:30:45.800
The lack of big predators has had an effect on all the animals of the Galapagos.

00:30:45.800 --> 00:30:50.800
They reproduce freely, so populations increase rapidly.

00:30:50.800 --> 00:31:07.800
And so consequently does evolutionary change.

00:31:07.800 --> 00:31:28.800
But island tameness has dangers.

00:31:28.800 --> 00:31:43.800
If a major predator does appear, then wildlife will be ill-equipped to defend itself.

00:31:43.800 --> 00:31:45.800
And one did.

00:31:45.800 --> 00:32:00.800
In the year 1535, the most successful predatory animal of all arrived. Man.

00:32:00.800 --> 00:32:06.800
By the beginning of the 17th century, the islands had become a haven for pirates.

00:32:06.800 --> 00:32:12.800
By the 19th century, whalers and mertens were calling here regularly.

00:32:12.800 --> 00:32:18.800
And all these ships had a disastrous effect.

00:32:18.800 --> 00:32:26.800
There is little or no fresh water on these islands, but they have a much rarer resource.

00:32:26.800 --> 00:32:32.800
Giant tortoises can survive without food or water for very long periods.

00:32:32.800 --> 00:32:37.800
So ships could come here, collect the tortoises, stow them in the hold,

00:32:37.800 --> 00:32:49.800
and after weeks at sea, bring one out, butcher it, and have a meal of fresh meat.

00:32:49.800 --> 00:32:58.800
Slow, lumbering, and with no way of defending themselves, the tortoises were easy victims.

00:32:58.800 --> 00:33:02.800
The population was decimated.

00:33:02.800 --> 00:33:10.800
By the 16th and 20th centuries, more than 100,000 were taken away and slaughtered.

00:33:10.800 --> 00:33:14.800
And the ships brought other dangers.

00:33:14.800 --> 00:33:19.800
Rats.

00:33:19.800 --> 00:33:30.800
They feasted on the tortoises' eggs.

00:33:30.800 --> 00:33:35.800
On the island of Pinson, rats consumed nearly every single egg,

00:33:35.800 --> 00:33:41.800
leaving the tortoise population on the brink of extinction.

00:33:41.800 --> 00:33:47.800
But another island lost its tortoise population altogether.

00:33:47.800 --> 00:33:52.800
Pinta, located on the shipping route around the northern fringe of the archipelago,

00:33:52.800 --> 00:33:56.800
was a favorite stopover for ships and their hungry crews.

00:33:56.800 --> 00:34:06.800
And the unique Pinta tortoise was presumed extinct by the early 20th century.

00:34:06.800 --> 00:34:12.800
But in 1972, an amazing discovery was made and filmed.

00:34:12.800 --> 00:34:18.800
A living male Pinta tortoise was discovered in the undergrowth.

00:34:18.800 --> 00:34:27.800
He was taken off to a protected enclosure on the main island to live out his days in comfort and safety.

00:34:27.800 --> 00:34:36.800
Here, he became an international celebrity, and he was given a name to reflect his state.

00:34:36.800 --> 00:34:41.800
Lonesome George.

00:34:41.800 --> 00:34:51.800
He's about 80 years old, and he's getting a bit creaky in his joints, as indeed am I.

00:34:51.800 --> 00:34:58.800
He is arguably the rarest animal in the world.

00:34:58.800 --> 00:35:04.800
Certainly, there can be none rarer, for he is the last of his kind.

00:35:04.800 --> 00:35:09.800
His female died a long, long time ago.

00:35:09.800 --> 00:35:16.800
When he dies, the Pinta species of Galapagos tortoise will be extinct.

00:35:16.800 --> 00:35:24.800
But he is a very important animal, probably more than any other single creature.

00:35:24.800 --> 00:35:31.800
He's focused the attention of the world on the fragility of our environment,

00:35:31.800 --> 00:35:44.800
and he stimulated science to look into whole new areas of research here in the Galapagos.

00:35:44.800 --> 00:35:52.800
Just 14 days after we filmed Lonesome, he died in his sleep.

00:35:52.800 --> 00:35:58.800
But he's not forgotten.

00:35:58.800 --> 00:36:04.800
Lonesome George's story, like Darwin's fleeting but famous visit 200 years ago,

00:36:04.800 --> 00:36:19.800
has attracted many visitors to the islands.

00:36:19.800 --> 00:36:25.800
Today, the archipelago is the basis of a multimillion dollar tourist industry.

00:36:25.800 --> 00:36:29.800
30,000 people live here in three small towns,

00:36:29.800 --> 00:36:38.800
and fleets of small boats take visitors on carefully planned trips to see the islands' main sights.

00:36:38.800 --> 00:36:45.800
It took animals and plants millions of years to find and colonize the Galapagos.

00:36:45.800 --> 00:36:54.800
Now, new species of animals and plants from the world's continents can get here in a matter of hours.

00:36:54.800 --> 00:37:03.800
And having got here, they're often inadvertently spread almost immediately through the islands.

00:37:03.800 --> 00:37:12.800
Seeds of foreign plants hitch a ride on the air currents generated by passing traffic.

00:37:12.800 --> 00:37:20.800
And the Galapagos' network of roads is now lined by foreign invaders.

00:37:20.800 --> 00:37:26.800
Among them, the common European blackberry, a plant that can grow a centimeter a day

00:37:26.800 --> 00:37:30.800
and create impenetrable thickets four meters thick.

00:37:30.800 --> 00:37:43.800
They not only choke and kill native plants, they even block the paths of large animals, such as the tortoises.

00:37:43.800 --> 00:37:54.800
Scientists are now trying to analyze the impact of human beings on the course of evolution in the islands.

00:37:54.800 --> 00:38:09.800
And surprisingly, perhaps, the finches that Darwin made famous are still providing new insights.

00:38:09.800 --> 00:38:18.800
Geologist Andrew Hendry is looking at how the finches' evolution may have been affected by human settlements.

00:38:18.800 --> 00:38:23.800
When humans come into a new location, essentially what they do is they change the environment.

00:38:23.800 --> 00:38:30.800
And that's changing selection that's acting on the populations.

00:38:30.800 --> 00:38:38.800
Hendry is studying one particular species, the medium ground fringe.

00:38:38.800 --> 00:38:47.800
And remarkably, he's found that this fringe, in its natural setting, is on the verge of dividing into two separate species.

00:38:47.800 --> 00:38:51.800
The two are defined by the size of their beaks.

00:38:51.800 --> 00:38:55.800
One is small, the other large.

00:38:55.800 --> 00:39:00.800
The difference between them has been caused by the types of food they eat.

00:39:00.800 --> 00:39:04.800
So if you feed on some small seed, then you tend to have a small beak.

00:39:04.800 --> 00:39:11.800
And if you feed on a large seed, then you tend to have a really big beak and you tend to have a hard bite.

00:39:11.800 --> 00:39:31.800
Remarkably, Hendry has found that among medium ground finches that live near human beings, the distinct big and small beak forms are getting fewer.

00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:41.800
It's as if the two variants are here merging back into one.

00:39:41.800 --> 00:39:49.800
The presence of human beings has stopped this finch from evolving.

00:39:49.800 --> 00:39:57.800
We found that they feed a lot on human foods, ranging from rice to fruit to grains to potato chips.

00:39:57.800 --> 00:40:04.800
Feeding on those types of different foods doesn't really seem to matter what your beak size is anymore.

00:40:04.800 --> 00:40:11.800
So there isn't that pressure to have a small beak version and a large beak version, and there's no selection against those intermediate birds anymore.

00:40:11.800 --> 00:40:14.800
There's plenty of foods for them to eat.

00:40:14.800 --> 00:40:17.800
So it seems like humans have caused the speciation reversal.

00:40:17.800 --> 00:40:28.800
They're fusing back together again as a result of human influences.

00:40:28.800 --> 00:40:39.800
So human beings can be just as much a part of nature as the forces that first shaped these islands and the organisms that live on them.

00:40:39.800 --> 00:40:47.800
But human beings can not only destroy, they can conserve.

00:40:47.800 --> 00:40:52.800
In the 1970s, the tortoise population reached an all-time low.

00:40:52.800 --> 00:40:56.800
There were only a few thousand of them left.

00:40:56.800 --> 00:41:08.800
But now there's a major breeding program for them.

00:41:08.800 --> 00:41:13.800
The tortoise population today is increasing.

00:41:13.800 --> 00:41:26.800
Once threatened species have been brought back from the brink.

00:41:26.800 --> 00:41:35.800
And scientists are discovering just how important and influential this reintroduction program might be.

00:41:35.800 --> 00:41:49.800
On Alcedo volcano, home for the largest population of free-roaming giant tortoises, a study has shown that they're crucial to the health of all the surrounding wildlife.

00:41:49.800 --> 00:41:59.800
Today there are some 15,000 tortoises on the Galapagos, and they're an essential element in the island's ecosystem.

00:41:59.800 --> 00:42:05.800
One study in particular has shown just how important they are.

00:42:05.800 --> 00:42:14.800
Biologist Steve Blake uses satellite tags to track their movements.

00:42:14.800 --> 00:42:25.800
They reveal to most people's great surprise that the tortoises migrate over huge distances, from the depths of the crater right up to the rim.

00:42:25.800 --> 00:42:27.800
It's just phenomenal.

00:42:27.800 --> 00:42:38.800
Why would a 600-pound reptile migrate from sea level to up to a thousand meters on some islands?

00:42:38.800 --> 00:42:49.800
From the air, the routes the tortoises take are clearly visible.

00:42:49.800 --> 00:42:59.800
And they use the same highways year after year.

00:42:59.800 --> 00:43:04.800
One of the fundamental drivers of the migration seems to be food.

00:43:04.800 --> 00:43:09.800
Tortoises tend to come to the highlands at the coolest time of year.

00:43:09.800 --> 00:43:14.800
Up in the highlands, they can feed on a year-round low-quality food source.

00:43:14.800 --> 00:43:24.800
But then when the rains kick in, the lowlands tend to green up, and the tortoises go down there, probably to fatten up.

00:43:24.800 --> 00:43:30.800
But Blake's studies reveal much more about the tortoises than just where they go.

00:43:30.800 --> 00:43:41.800
They demonstrate the extraordinary effect that tortoises have on their surrounding environment.

00:43:41.800 --> 00:43:48.800
They create special conditions that suit all kinds of other animals.

00:43:48.800 --> 00:43:52.800
They shape and prune the landscape.

00:43:52.800 --> 00:44:00.800
They disperse seeds, travel down the undergrowth and trim the lowest branches of the bushes.

00:44:00.800 --> 00:44:05.800
And all that allows seeds to germinate.

00:44:05.800 --> 00:44:11.800
It disturbs insects so that they can be gathered by hungry predators.

00:44:11.800 --> 00:44:18.800
Their dung is also crucial to the survival of many other creatures.

00:44:18.800 --> 00:44:28.800
Beetles lay their eggs in it, and their larvae grow fat on the nutriment that it still contains.

00:44:28.800 --> 00:44:32.800
Tortoises are definitely the megafauna of the highlands.

00:44:32.800 --> 00:44:35.800
They're like the elephants of Galapagos.

00:44:35.800 --> 00:44:38.800
They're out there in large numbers. They've got big bodies.

00:44:38.800 --> 00:44:43.800
They're creating myriad microhabitats for other species.

00:44:43.800 --> 00:44:46.800
They truly are gardeners of Galapagos.

00:44:46.800 --> 00:44:57.800
That's probably the best way to sum up their ecological role.

00:44:57.800 --> 00:45:00.800
The implications are very important.

00:45:00.800 --> 00:45:06.800
They suggest that the reintroduction of tortoises to islands where their numbers have been seriously reduced

00:45:06.800 --> 00:45:12.800
could restore the richness of the whole environment.

00:45:12.800 --> 00:45:29.800
We have the chance to bring back the full glory of these fragile ecosystems.

00:45:29.800 --> 00:45:33.800
Today, unlike many other tropical islands elsewhere,

00:45:33.800 --> 00:45:41.800
95% of Galapagos' biodiversity still survives.

00:45:41.800 --> 00:45:49.800
Just.

00:45:49.800 --> 00:45:59.800
And amazingly, new species are still being discovered.

00:45:59.800 --> 00:46:17.800
One was found just 35 miles north of Alcedo on the giant little visited volcano, Wolf.

00:46:17.800 --> 00:46:20.800
To get to Wolf, you really need a helicopter.

00:46:20.800 --> 00:46:27.800
It's one of the most hostile and least explored parts of the whole archipelago.

00:46:27.800 --> 00:46:39.800
There had been rumors of something strange living up on these remote high slopes.

00:46:39.800 --> 00:46:56.800
Something that lived in burrows and only emerged every now and then to feed.

00:46:56.800 --> 00:47:19.800
A scientific team went up to investigate.

00:47:19.800 --> 00:47:26.800
What they discovered astonished everybody.

00:47:26.800 --> 00:47:33.800
A completely new and unknown species of reptile.

00:47:33.800 --> 00:47:42.800
A pink iguana.

00:47:42.800 --> 00:47:50.800
Until now, it was thought that the Galapagos possessed only three species of iguana.

00:47:50.800 --> 00:47:54.800
The black marine iguana that lives on the seashore.

00:47:54.800 --> 00:48:04.800
And two species of yellow iguana that live in land feeding on cacti and other vegetation.

00:48:04.800 --> 00:48:13.800
This land iguana is certainly the most closely related to the newly discovered one.

00:48:13.800 --> 00:48:25.800
Genetic studies of the hundred or so individuals that make up this tiny population have shown that it diverged from its land iguana cousins more than five million years ago.

00:48:25.800 --> 00:48:36.800
So amazingly, it has been here just as long as the other two, but has remained unknown to science until now.

00:48:36.800 --> 00:48:50.800
The discovery also means that it is even older than Wolf Volcano, where it now lives.

00:48:50.800 --> 00:48:59.800
And of course, there is another great mystery that no one has yet explained.

00:48:59.800 --> 00:49:02.800
Nobody knows why it's pink.

00:49:02.800 --> 00:49:08.800
Could it be that to be pink up here brings something good?

00:49:08.800 --> 00:49:10.800
We don't know.

00:49:10.800 --> 00:49:19.800
Maybe this was once spread widely over the island and this is just the relic population that's left.

00:49:19.800 --> 00:49:22.800
Again, we don't know.

00:49:22.800 --> 00:49:25.800
Goodbye.

00:49:25.800 --> 00:49:28.800
But there's one thing that is quite certain.

00:49:28.800 --> 00:49:37.800
And that is that there's a lot we have yet to learn about the enchanted islands and about the animals that have evolved here.

00:49:37.800 --> 00:49:49.800
And one thing I have no doubt, Charles Darwin would be delighted.

00:49:49.800 --> 00:49:57.800
In just a few million years, this empty expanse of ocean was transformed.

00:49:57.800 --> 00:50:10.800
A series of volcanoes broke the surface and built the islands.

00:50:10.800 --> 00:50:23.800
Against heavy odds, a few species managed to reach them.

00:50:23.800 --> 00:50:32.800
They adapted to what they found.

00:50:32.800 --> 00:50:54.800
And so evolved into a multitude of new species.

00:50:54.800 --> 00:51:06.800
Each new discovery we make gives Darwin's theory a greater relevance.

00:51:06.800 --> 00:51:14.800
But beyond the strange plants and bizarre animals, there is a greater significance.

00:51:14.800 --> 00:51:21.800
What we've learned here has given us a greater understanding of our planet.

00:51:21.800 --> 00:51:31.800
And this small group of islands has revealed in microcosm the processes that have shaped all life on Earth.
